# LGD Integration — {{AGENT_NAME}} ↔ LabGrimoire Desktop {{AGENT_EMOJI}}

> **Optional pairing.** This file is a starting point for connecting your
> Ghost In Shell agent to LabGrimoire Desktop (LGD) — a local-first AI app
> that runs LLMs, tools, and a brain visualizer on your machine.

---

## Why pair?

| Without LGD | With LGD |
|-------------|----------|
| One CLI runtime per session | Headless agent calls into LGD's tool registry |
| Memory lives only in `memory/` | LGD provides a brain visualizer and write CLIs |
| No local LLM fallback | LGD ships with local-model option (Gemma, etc.) |
| Sanctum sources stay degraded | LGD write CLIs upgrade `extended_degraded` → `extended` |

The pairing is **optional**. The framework works perfectly without LGD.

---

## Setup

### 1. Install LabGrimoire Desktop

Download from: <https://github.com/cyhsieh817/labgrimoire-desktop>
(replace with the actual repository when published)

LGD installs to `~/Library/Application Support/labgrimoire/` on macOS.

### 2. Verify CLI

```bash
lgd --version          # confirm CLI is on PATH
lgd brain:status       # confirm brain DB is reachable
```

### 3. Bridge from this workspace

If `scripts/lgd_bridge.py` exists in your workspace (see
`examples/multi_cli_memory/scripts/lgd_bridge.py`), you can call LGD's
LLM + tools headlessly:

```bash
python3 scripts/lgd_bridge.py "Summarize today's session log"
python3 scripts/lgd_bridge.py --model gemma-4-26b "Build a report"
python3 scripts/lgd_bridge.py --max-turns 20 "Long task"
```

---

## Sanctum Upgrades

Once LGD is paired, edit `memory/fact_governance.yml` → `sanctum_registry`
and replace each `lgd_write_cli: null` with the real CLI command:

```yaml
sources:
  - id: episodic
    lgd_write_cli: "lgd brain:write-episode"
  - id: kanban
    lgd_write_cli: "lgd kanban:add"
  # …
```

After this change `python3 scripts/sanctum_audit.py` will re-check writes
against the canonical CLI and surface bypasses.

---

## Brain Visualization

LGD's brain visualizer reads `memory/brain_region_manifest.yml`. Run:

```bash
bash scripts/memory_region_refresh_tick.sh
```

…then open LGD → Brain. Each region shows the files that live there
(core / on-demand) and recent activity heat.

---

## Capability Matrix

| Capability | Source of truth |
|------------|-----------------|
| LLM call | LGD config (`grimoire.toml`) |
| Tool registry | LGD `tools/` directory |
| Brain DB | LGD chat.db + this workspace's `memory/` |
| Workflow runner | LGD `workflow/` |
| Skill catalog | this workspace's `memory/skill_dispatch.yml` (if present) |

---

## When NOT to use LGD

- If you only run one CLI (Claude Code) and don't need a local LLM, skip it.
- If you'd rather not have a desktop app, skip it.
- If you're on a server / headless machine, skip it.

The `lgd_write_cli: null` rows in the sanctum registry simply stay degraded.

